course: history
lesson: 59
title: China: modern China, space and contested topics
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Since about 1980 China has changed faster than almost any country in history. Mei's grandmother grew up without electricity; Mei rides a high-speed train that goes faster than a racing car, and China has landed on the far side of the Moon and on Mars and built its own space station.
explain: Mei is proud and sometimes says too much: «richest country next year», «ended poverty», «found life on Mars». The teacher checks: the World Bank numbers on poverty are real and enormous, the rover found signs of ancient water, not life, and per person China is still a middle-income country.
explain: Some topics make people shout: Taiwan, Tibet, Tiananmen in 1989 and Hong Kong in 2019–2020. A good teacher does not pick a team. Like a referee who first tells the facts everyone saw, then says what each side claims, he names each topic as contested and lets families talk at home.
explain: Wise: be proud of real achievements, check the big words, and when views divide sharply, describe instead of shouting. Friends can disagree about politics and still share lunch.
fact: China's economy grew very fast from about 1980 to the 2010s; counted in US dollars it is now the world's second biggest, after the USA; but shared out among about 1.4 billion people it is much less per person than in rich countries, so China is still a middle-income country, and growth has slowed in recent years.
fact: The World Bank estimates that more than 800 million people in China left extreme poverty since 1980; in 2021 the government declared extreme poverty ended by its own measure, though by the measures used for richer countries many people are still poor.
fact: China's high-speed rail network, over 40,000 km long, is larger than the rest of the world's high-speed lines combined, by most reports.
fact: Yang Liwei became China's first astronaut in 2003, making China the third country, after the USSR and the USA, to send people to space on its own rockets.
fact: Chang'e 4 made the first soft landing on the far side of the Moon in January 2019, and Chang'e 6 brought back the first samples from the far side in 2024.
fact: In 2021 China's Tianwen-1 mission orbited Mars and landed the Zhurong rover, which studied the ground and found signs of ancient water; no life was found.
fact: China completed its Tiangong space station, whose name means «Heavenly Palace», in 2022; Chinese astronauts live there in turns.
fact: Taiwan has been governed separately since 1949 by the Republic of China, with its own elected government; the People's Republic considers it part of China, and most countries do not formally recognise Taiwan as a state but keep informal ties. Its status is contested.
fact: Tibet came under the control of the People's Republic in 1950–1951; the Chinese government says Tibet has been part of China for centuries, while the Dalai Lama and many Tibetans abroad describe it as historically independent or self-governing. This is contested.
fact: In spring 1989 students and workers protested in Beijing's Tiananmen Square; on 3–4 June the army cleared the area and many people were killed. The number is unknown and disputed, the government says it restored order, and the event is not openly discussed in mainland China.
fact: In 2019 large protests in Hong Kong began against a bill that would have allowed suspects to be sent to mainland China for trial; in 2020 Beijing introduced a national security law. Supporters say it restored order, critics say it cut freedoms; views are strongly divided.
exercise: How fast has China's economy grown since about 1980? | Very fast, among the fastest in history | Slowly, about as fast as most of Europe | It shrank almost every year after 1980 | Only fast since the Olympics in 2008
exercise: What is the difference between a big economy and a rich people? | A big economy can still be middle-income per person | There is no difference: a big economy means rich people | A big economy only means a country has lots of land | A rich people always means a big, crowded country
exercise: Which organisation measured China's poverty reduction? | The World Bank | FIFA | The Olympic Committee | The Red Cross
exercise: About how many people in China left extreme poverty since 1980? | More than 800 million | About 8 million people | Fewer than 80 thousand | Only the people of Beijing
exercise: How long is China's high-speed rail network? | Over 40,000 km | About 400 km | About 4,000 km | Over 400,000 km long
exercise: Why are fast trains useful in a big country? | They link far-apart cities in a few hours | They are cheaper to ride than walking anywhere | They need no tracks and go across fields | They fly straight over the highest mountains
exercise: Who was China's first astronaut? | Yang Liwei | Yuri Gagarin | Neil Armstrong | Zheng He
exercise: Which country was China in sending people to space on its own rockets? | The third, after the USSR and the USA | The first, before both the USSR and the USA | The tenth, after India and Japan | The second, before America but after the USSR
exercise: What did Chang'e 4 do first in 2019? | A soft landing on the Moon's far side | The first landing of a Chinese rover on Mars | Bringing the first Moon rocks back to China | Building the first part of the Tiangong station
exercise: What did Chang'e 6 bring back? | The first samples from the Moon's far side | The first rocks ever brought back from Mars | Ice from the Moon's north pole to drink | The first Chinese astronaut from the Moon
exercise: What was the name of China's Mars rover? | Zhurong | Curiosity | Yutu-1 | Tiangong
exercise: What does «Tiangong» mean? | Heavenly Palace | Red Dragon | Long March | Moon Rabbit
exercise: What facts about Taiwan do both sides agree on? | It has been governed separately since 1949 with its own elected government | It has been ruled directly from Beijing since 1949, with no government of its own | It was a province of Japan until 1997, when it joined the United Nations | It has had no government at all since 1949, only a Japanese governor
exercise: Why do historians say Tibet's status in different centuries is complicated? | Its ties to China changed over time and the sides describe them differently | Nobody lived there before 1950, so there are no old records of any kind to look at | It is on another continent, so Chinese and Indian books never mention it | It was a British colony for 500 years and the British papers were lost
exercise: What happened in Tiananmen Square in spring 1989? | Students and workers protested, and the army cleared the area in June | A new emperor was crowned there in a big ceremony, with soldiers in red | The Olympic Games opened there with a huge parade of athletes | Farmers held a market festival there and nothing else happened
exercise: What was the 2019 Hong Kong bill about? | Sending suspects to mainland China for trial | Banning rice from every shop in Hong Kong | Building a long bridge from Hong Kong to Taiwan | Changing the flag colour from red to blue
exercise: What did Beijing introduce in Hong Kong in 2020? | A national security law | A new language for schools | A ban on all trains and buses | A second currency for Beijing
test: Mei says China will be the richest country in the world next year. What should she check? | The difference between total size and wealth per person | Nothing: if the economy is big, every person is rich | Only how many people live in China's biggest cities | Which country has more pandas and more high-speed trains
test: The teacher calls China's poverty reduction «only propaganda». Who is mostly right? | Mei: World Bank numbers show it is real and huge | The teacher: the World Bank just copied China's own numbers | Neither: there was never any poverty in China to reduce | Both: it happened, but only for people living in Beijing
test: Mei says China sent a man to space before America. What is the honest version? | China was third, in 2003; the USSR and the USA went in 1961 | Mei is right: China sent its first astronaut up in 1950 | China has never sent anyone to space, only robots and probes | China and America sent their first astronauts on the same day
test: OMG mei says the chinese rover found aliens on mars!! | No; it found signs of ancient water, which is not life | yes!! little green men waved at the camera lol | Yes, but they ran away before it took a photo | No, coz the rover crashed and never landed at all
test: The teacher thought the ISS was the only space station. What did Mei teach him? | China has its own station, Tiangong, finished in 2022 | The ISS itself was built and is owned only by China | There are no space stations at all, only rockets | Tiangong is a Chinese station built on the Moon in 2022
test: Mei says «Taiwan is a province of China»; a classmate shouts «Taiwan is a country!». What did the teacher wisely do? | Stop the shouting, explain both views and the undisputed facts | Give Mei a prize, because she said it first and loudest | Send the classmate out of the room for shouting at Mei | Tell the class that one side is stupid and move on to the next topic
test: If someone says «Tibet was always part of China» and another says «Tibet was always free», what is the fair reply? | Both are positions; it is contested and history was complicated | The first is a plain fact that every historian in the world agrees with | The second is a plain fact that every historian agrees with | Neither matters, because nobody lived in Tibet before 1950
test: Mei says nothing happened in 1989. What is widely documented? | Protests in Tiananmen Square ended by the army, with many killed | Nothing at all: she is right, and all the photos and films are fakes | A big football final in Tiananmen Square watched by millions | A new dynasty began, with an emperor crowned in the square
test: Why might some events not be taught in every country? | Governments choose what schools teach, and some topics are sensitive | All events are taught in every country, so this never really happens | Nobody remembers anything that happened more than ten years ago | Textbooks always run out of pages before the last hundred years
test: Mei says the Hong Kong protests were caused by foreigners. How should a teacher answer? | Describe the events, the bill and the law, and say views divide | Agree with Mei completely and say nothing more about it in class | Say Mei is a liar in front of the whole class | Refuse to say that anything happened in Hong Kong
test: In a heated class argument about politics, what keeps friends friends? | Facts first, name the dispute, no insults | Whoever shouts loudest wins | Never talk to each other again | Pretend nothing exists
exam: Identify the position of the People's Republic of China among states that have sent persons into space by their own means. | The third, following the USSR and the United States | The first, preceding both the USSR and the United States | The second, preceding the United States by two years | The fifth, following India, Japan and the United States
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the achievement of the Chang'e 4 mission in January 2019? | The first soft landing upon the far side of the Moon | The first landing of persons upon the Moon | The first return of samples from Mars | The completion of the Tiangong station
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the governance of Taiwan since 1949. | It has been governed separately with its own elected government, its status being contested | It has been administered directly from Beijing, with no elected government of its own | It has been a colony of Britain, administered from Hong Kong until the year 1997 | It has been governed by Japan, its status being settled by a treaty of 1949
exam: Ascertain the finding of the Zhurong rover in respect of the planet Mars. | Signs of ancient water, but no evidence of life | Conclusive evidence of living micro-organisms | Rivers of liquid water flowing at present | The remains of an ancient settlement
